 ],
 "literal": "Alas! if I say, I fear the tiger / If I embrace and take him, his wide chest I cannot lift / Like me, may you suffer a great trembling / O merciless Death who brought this painful state! / Holding my forearm adorned with conch bangles, / walk a little, let us reach the mountain shade.",
 "translation": "If I cry out \"Alas!\", I fear the tiger will come. If I try to lift him in my embrace, I cannot lift his broad chest. O merciless Death, who brought this painful end, may you suffer a great trembling just like me! My love, hold my forearm with its conch bangles; walk just a little, and let us reach the shade of the mountain.",
 "modern": "\"ஐயோ\" என்று நான் கதறினால், புலி வந்துவிடுமோ என்று அஞ்சுகிறேன். அவனது அகன்ற மார்பைத் தழுவித் தூக்க முயன்றால், என்னால் தூக்க முடியவில்லை. எங்களுக்குத் துன்பம் தந்த இரக்கமற்ற கூற்றுவனே (எமனே), நீயும் என்னைப்போலவே பெரு நடுக்கத்தை அடைவாயாக! என் வளையல் அணிந்த முன்கையைப் பற்றிக்கொண்டு, சிறிது தூரம் நடந்து அந்த மலை நிழலை அடைவோம், எழுந்து வா.",
 "explanation": "This poignant poem belongs to the Poduviyal tiṇai and the Mudupālai tuṟai, depicting a wife's lament over her dying or deceased husband in the harsh desert wilderness. The speaker is the wife, who finds herself in an agonizing dilemma. If she screams in grief, she fears attracting predatory tigers; yet she lacks the physical strength to lift and carry her husband's broad, heavy chest. In her despair, she curses Death (Kūṟṟu) for its merciless cruelty, wishing it would experience the same violent trembling of grief that she feels. Finally, speaking to her husband as if he were still alive, she begs him to hold her bangled forearm and walk just a little further to the safety of the mountain shade.",
